A framebuffer fetch from a compressed (UBWC) attachment reads it from a pass of its own (2026-11-16, darwin-lisa
session 13). Applies after hakios-metal-x-ubwc-rt.patch.

S9-B (metal_ubwc_rt=1) drew the glass right from compressed offscreen targets read by later passes, and lost the
Control Center's rounded corners: the clip CoreAnimation draws into a memoryless target and reads back by framebuffer
fetch in the same pass read as if never drawn. A compressed target read after its pass's tail (colour cache flushed,
CACHE_FLUSH_TS) is the one way the phone has shown it read right; mid-pass the fetch went through the in-pass barrier
(colour cache flushed, CACHE_INVALIDATE) alone. So a fetching draw into a pass whose attachments include a compressed
one does not join that pass: the pass is closed and the draw opens the next, with Load. A linear target keeps the
barrier and its single pass.

Measured on the host: render_test a_fetch_from_a_compressed_target_reads_it_from_a_pass_of_its_own (2 passes for
compressed, 1 for linear); 267 tests, the same 14 pre-existing failed checks.

